GLM-5.3-Flash (glm5next) CPU library, step 3 of 5: content-verified cache of pooled indexer keys.
NOT deployed. Exact by construction. GGML_CPU_GLM_POOL_CACHE=1, default off. Apply after glm5next-kpool-wide.patch.
Result: CPU library sha256 5e3ef3b3de7fbe37d24a04afa7d3bf8b66308f259deb6533aa0b6e64c82927a2.

A pool's summary depends only on its four member key/gate rows and the position weights. The cache reuses a stored summary
only after a byte comparison of every input that determines it (the four F16 rows, the F32 weight rows, operand order, FP
control state); source addresses are lookup hints, never authority. Misses run the existing arithmetic. Records are owned per
worker, bounded per worker (64 MiB) and per process (4 GiB); allocation failure falls back to computing. It still READS every
member row each token to compare it, so the cost stays O(context) -- a position-tracked cache would not, and is not built.

Evidence:
  standalone (engineering/2026-09-20/archive/serving/fleet-0912-ctx/glm-pool-cache-r2-0920/README.md): 12 stateful arms at 2 and
  15 workers, 301,517,504 saved bytes per arm, all equal, including edits, restore, resized graphs, eviction, forced 1 MiB limit.
  four-NUMA pooling chain, 11 layers, cached-graph medians: 5.71x at 1,026 pools, 4.14x at 8,194, 2.30x at 25,002. First fills are slower.
  full model, window w1 2026-09-20, same loaded process, actual Codex requests through the Paseo wrapper, greedy, 3,998-token input,
  four runs per mode in ABCCBA ABCCBA order, generated text and speculation counters identical with the cache on and off:
      kernels without the cache 17.05 / 17.24 / 17.30 / 17.30   mean 17.225 tok/s
      kernels with the cache    17.80 / 17.66 / 17.51 / 17.48   mean 17.613 tok/s   (+2.3%)
  No long-context model measurement exists yet for the cache.

